            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA reports the rover has uncovered evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick rock stack on the rim of Jezero Crater was built by repeated ancient asteroid impacts. The area, called the “Broom Point member,” contains layered bedrock likely more than 3.9 billion years old, giving scientists a record of violent impact processes early in Mars’ history【5:2†source】.\n\nA second recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km. It reached that mark on sol 1,890 of the mission, after about five years and four months of driving. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter also imaged the rover and its tracks from orbit shortly before the milestone【5:0†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active beyond Jezero Crater’s rim, investigating ancient rocks and terrain while continuing to set mobility milestones on Mars.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/mars/stories/",
                    "start_index": 400,
                    "end_index": 412,
                    "title": "Mars: News & Features - NASA Science"
